the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>3$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>3$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(y-3)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>3$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(y-3)^{2}$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "244419de-9b13-5923-b0a5-1bdc6f9b03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bde1f724-b9ed-5e79-a94a-02a1a6dfc100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461941} {:answer/id #uuid "e23ece2c-ad5c-5961-89b9-83eae7e540a2" :answer/type {:db/id